    "excerpt": "Early pancreatic cancer may produce no symptoms. When symptoms appear, they are usually non-specific: pain, weight loss, indigestion or fatigue can have many causes. It is the combination, course and context—not a single symptom—that determines whether pancreatic investigation is needed.",
    "content": "**Early pancreatic cancer may produce no symptoms.** When symptoms appear, they are usually non-specific: pain, weight loss, indigestion or fatigue can have many causes. It is the *combination, course and context*—not a single symptom—that determines whether pancreatic investigation is needed.\n\n## The 15 symptoms clinicians ask about\n\n1. Persistent epigastric discomfort or aching upper abdomen.\n2. Pain reaching the middle back or affecting sleep.\n3. Unintended weight loss without dietary change.\n4. Appetite reduction or food aversion.\n5. Early satiety and uncomfortable fullness.\n6. Yellow eyes or skin (jaundice).\n7. Dark urine unexplained by dehydration or drugs.\n8. Pale or clay-coloured stools.\n9. Persistent itching with cholestatic features.\n10. Greasy, oily, floating or foul stools (possible malabsorption).\n11. Persistent nausea or vomiting.\n12. New diabetes, particularly with unintentional weight loss.\n13. Unexplained deterioration in previously stable blood glucose.\n14. Fatigue and declining physical condition.\n15. Unprovoked blood clots (leg swelling/pain or pulmonary embolism symptoms).\n\nBloating, diarrhoea, constipation and mood changes can also occur; none independently diagnoses cancer.\n\n## Why the location of a tumour matters\n\nThe **head of the pancreas** lies near the bile duct: a mass here may obstruct bile drainage, leading to jaundice, dark urine, pale stools and itching. **Body/tail tumours** may not cause jaundice and can cause vague pain or weight loss. Neither pattern is definitive.\n\n## Red flags requiring action\n\n**Emergency care:** jaundice with fever/chills and abdominal pain, collapse, sudden severe pain, inability to hydrate, vomiting blood or black stools, or sudden chest pain/shortness of breath.\n\n**Prompt medical review:** otherwise unexplained jaundice; pain that becomes persistent plus weight loss; new or rapidly changing diabetes accompanied by weight loss; new unexplained symptoms in a person with strong family history or chronic pancreatitis.\n\nThe UK NICE guideline highlights adults 40+ with jaundice and adults 60+ with weight loss plus certain digestive symptoms or new diabetes for urgent evaluation. Indian clinicians should apply individual assessment and local care pathways, rather than use these thresholds as universal rules.\n\n## What it could be instead\n\nAcid reflux, functional dyspepsia, H. pylori gastritis/ulcer, gallstones, bile-duct stones, hepatitis, pancreatitis, medication effects and other abdominal diseases may overlap. Clinicians may order liver chemistry, glucose/HbA1c, selected blood tests and appropriate imaging. A normal serum amylase, lipase or CA 19-9 is **not reassurance that cancer has been excluded**.\n\n**Next:** [Read the full differential guide](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-vs-acidity), [blood test limitations](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-blood-tests-imaging-biopsy) and [jaundice pathway](/blog/jaundice-dark-urine-pale-stools-pancreas-liver).\n\n## Evidence specific to this topic\n\n- [NICE NG12](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng12/chapter/recommendations-organised-by-site-of-cancer)\n- [NCI symptoms](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/symptoms)\n- [NCI risks](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/causes-risk-factors)\n\n## QXL Diagnostics: test availability, TAT and enquiries\n\n- **Test availability:** Test available at QXL Diagnostics. Confirm the individual requested test when enquiring.\n- **Turnaround time (TAT):** Same Day (indicative; individual tests and sample cut-offs must be confirmed).\n- **Test details and unconfirmed information:** Contact QXL Diagnostics Super Speciality Lab Bengaluru.\n- **WhatsApp for Details:** [Message QXL Diagnostics](https://wa.me/919964639639?text=Hello%20QXL%20Diagnostics%2C%20please%20share%20the%20test%20details%2C%20sample%20requirements%2C%20current%20price%20and%20confirmed%20turnaround%20time%20for%20pancreatic%20or%20gastrointestinal%20laboratory%20tests.).\n- **Phone:** [+91 99646 39639](tel:+919964639639).\n\n**Please note:** Same Day is an indicative laboratory turnaround, subject to sample receipt/cut-off, individual assay workflow, and confirmation by QXL. Some specialised and stool tests may take longer. Urgent symptoms require clinical care, not waiting for a result. This service is for clinician-directed laboratory assessment. No laboratory package screens for, diagnoses, or rules out pancreatic cancer.\n\n## Medical sources and evidence\n\n- [NCI symptoms](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/symptoms)\n- [NCI diagnosis](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/diagnosis)\n\n\n## Questions patients frequently ask\n\n### What is the most common first symptom?\n\nThere is no single reliable first symptom. Many patients have vague pain, weight loss or no early symptoms.\n\n### Can pancreatic cancer occur without jaundice?\n\nYes. Jaundice depends partly on whether a tumour obstructs bile drainage.\n\n### Does back pain always mean pancreatic cancer?\n\nNo. Musculoskeletal pain is much more common; persistent back pain with weight loss or abdominal symptoms warrants clinical assessment.\n",
