    "excerpt": "CA 19-9 is an oncology adjunct, not a general pancreatic cancer screening test. It measures a circulating carbohydrate antigen associated with biliary and pancreatic disease, among other conditions. A single result does not diagnose or exclude cancer.",
    "content": "**CA 19-9 is an oncology adjunct, not a general pancreatic cancer screening test.** It measures a circulating carbohydrate antigen associated with biliary and pancreatic disease, among other conditions. A single result does not diagnose or exclude cancer.\n\n## When is CA 19-9 useful?\n\nSpecialists may use it as part of a baseline assessment in a patient with a strongly suspected or established pancreatic malignancy, and for serial monitoring of treatment response or disease course when the marker is informative. It does **not** replace CT, MRI/MRCP, EUS or tissue examination.\n\n## Can CA 19-9 be high without cancer?\n\nYes. Biliary obstruction, cholangitis, pancreatitis and some benign hepatobiliary conditions can elevate CA 19-9. Other cancers can also increase it. Interpretation in jaundiced patients can be particularly misleading. Clinicians may interpret or repeat a level after obstruction is addressed, guided by the clinical context.\n\n## Can CA 19-9 be normal despite cancer?\n\nYes. Small or early tumours may not cause an increase; some individuals with the Lewis antigen-negative phenotype cannot produce appreciable CA 19-9. Do not assume that a laboratory's printed reference interval defines the presence or absence of pancreatic cancer. Assay-specific reference ranges and methods matter.\n\n## When *not* to order it\n\nNot as an annual health check for an asymptomatic average-risk adult; not because of isolated routine acidity; not to reassure a patient with unexplained jaundice and weight loss; not as part of generic male/female tumour-marker screening panels without an established indication.\n\n## What should be assessed alongside CA 19-9?\n\nDepending on clinical presentation: total/direct bilirubin, ALP/GGT, AST/ALT and imaging status. If monitoring cancer, the treating team should correlate marker trends with imaging, clinical events, treatment dates and assay changes. Trending the same assay/lab where practicable improves comparability.\n\n## QXL testing and ordering\n\nQXL lists CA 19-9 in [oncology](https://qxldiagnostics.com/specialities/oncology) and [gastroenterology](https://qxldiagnostics.com/specialities/gastroenterology) services. **Clinician-directed ordering is recommended.** Indicative TAT: Same Day, subject to test-specific confirmation. For current price, sample details and final TAT, Contact QXL Diagnostics Super Speciality Lab Bengaluru.\n\n**Read also:** [Complete pancreatic diagnostic pathway](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-vs-acidity) and [blood tests, scans and biopsy](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-blood-tests-imaging-biopsy).\n\n## More references\n\n- [MedlinePlus CA 19-9](https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/ca-19-9-blood-test-pancreatic-cancer/)\n- [Mayo CA 19-9 assay](https://www.mayocliniclabs.com/test-catalog/Overview/9288)\n- [Indian consensus 2025](https://www.ijmpo.org/ejournals/topic/indian-consensus-guidelines-for-the-management-of-pancreatic-cancer)\n\n## QXL Diagnostics: test availability, TAT and enquiries\n\n- **Test availability:** Test available at QXL Diagnostics. Confirm the individual requested test when enquiring.\n- **Turnaround time (TAT):** Same Day (indicative; individual tests and sample cut-offs must be confirmed).\n- **Test details and unconfirmed information:** Contact QXL Diagnostics Super Speciality Lab Bengaluru.\n- **WhatsApp for Details:** [Message QXL Diagnostics](https://wa.me/919964639639?text=Hello%20QXL%20Diagnostics%2C%20please%20share%20the%20test%20details%2C%20sample%20requirements%2C%20current%20price%20and%20confirmed%20turnaround%20time%20for%20pancreatic%20or%20gastrointestinal%20laboratory%20tests.).\n- **Phone:** [+91 99646 39639](tel:+919964639639).\n\n**Please note:** Same Day is an indicative laboratory turnaround, subject to sample receipt/cut-off, individual assay workflow, and confirmation by QXL. Some specialised and stool tests may take longer. Urgent symptoms require clinical care, not waiting for a result. This service is for clinician-directed laboratory assessment. No laboratory package screens for, diagnoses, or rules out pancreatic cancer.\n\n## Medical sources and evidence\n\n- [NCI symptoms](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/symptoms)\n- [NCI diagnosis](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/diagnosis)\n\n\n## Questions patients frequently ask\n\n### What is the normal CA 19-9 range?\n\nUse the reference interval printed by the performing laboratory for its assay; there is no single result that confirms or excludes pancreatic cancer.\n\n### Can jaundice raise CA 19-9?\n\nYes. Bile-duct obstruction and related inflammation can cause elevations independent of cancer.\n\n### Is CA 19-9 a cancer screening test?\n\nNo. It is not recommended as a screening test in average-risk people without symptoms.\n",
