    "excerpt": "There is no routine blood-test panel that rules out pancreatic cancer. Investigations are layered according to symptoms and the doctor's initial findings.",
    "content": "There is **no routine blood-test panel that rules out pancreatic cancer**. Investigations are layered according to symptoms and the doctor's initial findings.\n\n## Step 1: history, examination and triage\n\nLook for jaundice, weight loss, progressive upper-abdominal/back pain, new diabetes, steatorrhoea, family history and acute illness. Jaundice with fever and significant abdominal pain may indicate ascending cholangitis: arrange emergency care, not routine lab collection.\n\n## Step 2: selective blood and stool tests\n\n| Investigation | Clinical role | Caution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total/direct bilirubin; ALP; GGT; AST; ALT | Pattern of cholestasis vs hepatocellular injury | Neither identifies a malignant cause nor locates obstruction |\n| CBC | Anaemia, inflammation and clinical status | Normal results do not exclude malignancy |\n| Renal function and electrolytes | Baseline status, dehydration, contrast-planning context | Not a cancer test |\n| Glucose / HbA1c | New or worsening diabetes | Most new diabetes is not cancer-associated |\n| Lipase ± amylase | Suspected acute pancreatitis | Neither screens for or excludes pancreatic cancer |\n| CA 19-9 | Selected oncological context; response trends | Non-specific and may be normal in cancer |\n| Faecal elastase | Evaluate suspected exocrine pancreatic insufficiency | Malabsorption test, not malignancy test |\n\n## Step 3: imaging the pancreas and biliary tree\n\n- **Ultrasound:** can detect duct dilatation, gallstones or liver findings; limited sensitivity for small pancreatic lesions.\n- **Multiphasic pancreas-protocol contrast CT:** central test for visualising suspected pancreatic tumour, vascular relationships and operability.\n- **MRI/MRCP:** complementary cross-sectional imaging for ducts, cystic lesions or indeterminate CT findings.\n- **EUS:** high-detail assessment of selected lesions and a route for tissue sampling.\n- **ERCP:** primarily to manage or sample biliary obstruction as indicated; carries procedural risks.\n- **PET-CT:** selective staging/clinical situations, not first-line primary diagnosis.\n\n## Step 4: pathology and multidisciplinary decisions\n\nBiopsy via EUS-guided sampling or another appropriate route can provide a histological diagnosis and material for molecular assessment. **Biopsy is generally necessary before systemic anticancer therapy**, but a specialist team may proceed to surgery without preoperative biopsy in a selected resectable case. Do not delay referral waiting for a generic test panel.\n\n## Step 5: after diagnosis\n\nThe multidisciplinary team evaluates stage, vascular involvement, treatment options, germline testing and, where appropriate, tumour profiling. Serial CA 19-9, liver tests, nutritional assessment and glucose monitoring can support ongoing management. QXL may contribute laboratory work within its verified test scope; imaging/procedures are performed by appropriately equipped clinical services.\n\n**Related:** [CA 19-9 explained](/blog/ca-19-9-test-pancreatic-cancer), [pancreatic symptoms](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-early-warning-signs).\n\n## Specific clinical guidance\n\n- [Indian consensus 2025](https://www.ijmpo.org/ejournals/topic/indian-consensus-guidelines-for-the-management-of-pancreatic-cancer)\n- [ESMO clinical practice](https://www.annalsofoncology.org/article/S0923-7534%2823%2900824-4/fulltext)\n- [NICE NG85](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng85/chapter/recommendations)\n- [ACS diagnostic tests](https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/pancreatic-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/how-diagnosed.html)\n\n## QXL Diagnostics: test availability, TAT and enquiries\n\n- **Test availability:** Test available at QXL Diagnostics. Confirm the individual requested test when enquiring.\n- **Turnaround time (TAT):** Same Day (indicative; individual tests and sample cut-offs must be confirmed).\n- **Test details and unconfirmed information:** Contact QXL Diagnostics Super Speciality Lab Bengaluru.\n- **WhatsApp for Details:** [Message QXL Diagnostics](https://wa.me/919964639639?text=Hello%20QXL%20Diagnostics%2C%20please%20share%20the%20test%20details%2C%20sample%20requirements%2C%20current%20price%20and%20confirmed%20turnaround%20time%20for%20pancreatic%20or%20gastrointestinal%20laboratory%20tests.).\n- **Phone:** [+91 99646 39639](tel:+919964639639).\n\n**Please note:** Same Day is an indicative laboratory turnaround, subject to sample receipt/cut-off, individual assay workflow, and confirmation by QXL. Some specialised and stool tests may take longer. Urgent symptoms require clinical care, not waiting for a result. This service is for clinician-directed laboratory assessment. No laboratory package screens for, diagnoses, or rules out pancreatic cancer.\n\n## Medical sources and evidence\n\n- [NCI symptoms](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/symptoms)\n- [NCI diagnosis](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/diagnosis)\n\n\n## Questions patients frequently ask\n\n### Can a normal LFT exclude pancreatic cancer?\n\nNo. A pancreatic tumour may exist without bile-duct obstruction.\n\n### Is ultrasound enough to rule out pancreatic cancer?\n\nNot when clinical suspicion remains high; CT, MRI/MRCP or EUS may be required.\n\n### Does everyone need a biopsy before surgery?\n\nNot always. The specialist MDT decides based on imaging, resectability and treatment plan.\n",
