    "excerpt": "New jaundice is not “ordinary acidity.” Yellow eyes/skin, dark urine, clay-coloured stools and itching may reflect obstructed bile flow or liver disease. Gallstones, viral hepatitis, medication injury, inflammation and malignancy are among many causes.",
    "content": "**New jaundice is not “ordinary acidity.”** Yellow eyes/skin, dark urine, clay-coloured stools and itching may reflect obstructed bile flow or liver disease. Gallstones, viral hepatitis, medication injury, inflammation and malignancy are among many causes.\n\n## Understanding the symptoms\n\nConjugated (direct) bilirubin can appear in urine, darkening it. Reduced bile pigment reaching the intestine can make stools pale. Bile salt and other factors may contribute to itching. A pancreatic head lesion can compress the common bile duct, but **jaundice alone does not establish a diagnosis of cancer**.\n\n## When to go to emergency\n\n**Fever/chills + jaundice + upper-abdominal pain** is concerning for cholangitis; confusion, low blood pressure or severe illness increases urgency. Emergency assessment and treatment should not wait for an online lab package.\n\n## Common investigations\n\nA clinician may order total and direct bilirubin, AST/ALT, ALP, GGT, CBC, renal profile and selected clotting tests depending on severity. A predominant ALP/GGT and conjugated bilirubin rise can suggest cholestasis, but the **cause and level of obstruction need imaging**. Ultrasound often assesses duct dilatation or stones first. Cross-sectional CT/MRCP and EUS may follow when appropriate. ERCP may relieve an obstructed duct.\n\n## How QXL fits in\n\nQXL lists liver biochemical testing in its gastroenterology services. The laboratory supports investigation, but blood tests and a normal CA 19-9 cannot replace urgent clinical evaluation for new unexplained jaundice.\n\n**Related:** [complete article](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-vs-acidity), [imaging guide](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-blood-tests-imaging-biopsy).\n\n## Further sources\n\n- [NCI diagnosis](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/diagnosis)\n- [NICE NG12](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng12/chapter/recommendations-organised-by-site-of-cancer)\n- [Indian consensus 2025](https://www.ijmpo.org/ejournals/topic/indian-consensus-guidelines-for-the-management-of-pancreatic-cancer)\n\n## QXL Diagnostics: test availability, TAT and enquiries\n\n- **Test availability:** Test available at QXL Diagnostics. Confirm the individual requested test when enquiring.\n- **Turnaround time (TAT):** Same Day (indicative; individual tests and sample cut-offs must be confirmed).\n- **Test details and unconfirmed information:** Contact QXL Diagnostics Super Speciality Lab Bengaluru.\n- **WhatsApp for Details:** [Message QXL Diagnostics](https://wa.me/919964639639?text=Hello%20QXL%20Diagnostics%2C%20please%20share%20the%20test%20details%2C%20sample%20requirements%2C%20current%20price%20and%20confirmed%20turnaround%20time%20for%20pancreatic%20or%20gastrointestinal%20laboratory%20tests.).\n- **Phone:** [+91 99646 39639](tel:+919964639639).\n\n**Please note:** Same Day is an indicative laboratory turnaround, subject to sample receipt/cut-off, individual assay workflow, and confirmation by QXL. Some specialised and stool tests may take longer. Urgent symptoms require clinical care, not waiting for a result. This service is for clinician-directed laboratory assessment. No laboratory package screens for, diagnoses, or rules out pancreatic cancer.\n\n## Medical sources and evidence\n\n- [NCI symptoms](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/symptoms)\n- [NCI diagnosis](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/diagnosis)\n\n\n## Questions patients frequently ask\n\n### Can gallstones cause jaundice and pale stools?\n\nYes, gallstones can obstruct the bile duct and produce the same pattern.\n\n### Can pancreatic cancer cause painless jaundice?\n\nYes; painless jaundice is one possible presentation, but other causes exist.\n\n### Will bilirubin tell whether obstruction is cancer?\n\nNo. Blood tests indicate a physiological pattern; imaging and specialist evaluation identify the cause.\n",
