    "excerpt": "Greasy, difficult-to-flush or floating stools can be caused by fat maldigestion, including pancreatic exocrine insufficiency (EPI), but there are many other causes of abnormal stool. EPI can occur with chronic pancreatitis, pancreatic surgery, pancreatic tumours and other conditions; a patient needs",
    "content": "Greasy, difficult-to-flush or floating stools can be caused by fat maldigestion, including **pancreatic exocrine insufficiency (EPI)**, but there are many other causes of abnormal stool. EPI can occur with chronic pancreatitis, pancreatic surgery, pancreatic tumours and other conditions; a patient needs a clinical assessment to determine the cause.\n\n## Why stool appearance changes\n\nThe pancreas supplies enzymes required to digest dietary fat. If enough digestive enzymes do not reach the intestine, a person may develop greasy or foul-smelling stool, bloating, excessive gas, diarrhoea, weight loss and nutritional deficiencies. One symptom by itself is not a diagnosis.\n\n## Faecal elastase-1: role and limitation\n\nFaecal elastase is an appropriate **initial test for suspected EPI**. It should be collected on a **semi-solid or solid stool specimen**: watery diarrhoea can dilute the result and yield misleading low values. Interpretation must consider the assay, clinical probability, stool consistency and other gastrointestinal causes. This test **does not detect or rule out pancreatic cancer**.\n\n## Further laboratory assessment\n\nClinicians may choose CBC, albumin, iron studies or targeted nutritional biomarkers depending on weight loss, diet, symptoms and examination. Automatically ordering all vitamins is not needed. If imaging is indicated for persistent pain, weight loss or jaundice, a normal or low faecal elastase result should not delay it.\n\n## QXL patient pathway\n\nQXL's gastroenterology page lists stool elastase and malabsorption investigations. The service team must confirm current availability, specimen transport, sample stability, pricing and referral requirements. A **clinician-directed EPI assessment** is more accurate than a “pancreatic cancer stool test” package.\n\n**Related:** [pancreatic symptoms](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-early-warning-signs), [blood tests and imaging](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-blood-tests-imaging-biopsy).\n\n## Evidence\n\n- [AGA exocrine insufficiency](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37737818/)\n\n## QXL Diagnostics: test availability, TAT and enquiries\n\n- **Test availability:** Test available at QXL Diagnostics. Confirm the individual requested test when enquiring.\n- **Turnaround time (TAT):** Same Day (indicative; individual tests and sample cut-offs must be confirmed).\n- **Test details and unconfirmed information:** Contact QXL Diagnostics Super Speciality Lab Bengaluru.\n- **WhatsApp for Details:** [Message QXL Diagnostics](https://wa.me/919964639639?text=Hello%20QXL%20Diagnostics%2C%20please%20share%20the%20test%20details%2C%20sample%20requirements%2C%20current%20price%20and%20confirmed%20turnaround%20time%20for%20pancreatic%20or%20gastrointestinal%20laboratory%20tests.).\n- **Phone:** [+91 99646 39639](tel:+919964639639).\n\n**Please note:** Same Day is an indicative laboratory turnaround, subject to sample receipt/cut-off, individual assay workflow, and confirmation by QXL. Some specialised and stool tests may take longer. Urgent symptoms require clinical care, not waiting for a result. This service is for clinician-directed laboratory assessment. No laboratory package screens for, diagnoses, or rules out pancreatic cancer.\n\n## Medical sources and evidence\n\n- [NCI symptoms](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/symptoms)\n- [NCI diagnosis](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/diagnosis)\n\n\n## Questions patients frequently ask\n\n### Is floating stool always pancreatic disease?\n\nNo. Stool can float for several benign reasons. Greasy, frequent stool with weight loss deserves clinical assessment.\n\n### Does low faecal elastase mean cancer?\n\nNo. It suggests possible exocrine insufficiency but does not identify the cause.\n\n### Can watery stool affect faecal elastase?\n\nYes. Excess water can dilute the sample and produce a falsely low measurement.\n",
