    "excerpt": "Upper-abdominal pain is a symptom, not a diagnosis. GERD, functional dyspepsia, gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, gallstones, pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer can overlap, so there is no reliable at-home pain test to distinguish them.",
    "content": "Upper-abdominal pain is a **symptom, not a diagnosis**. GERD, functional dyspepsia, gastritis, peptic ulcer disease, gallstones, pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer can overlap, so there is no reliable at-home pain test to distinguish them.\n\n## Pattern comparison\n\n| Condition | Common clue | A clue that changes the plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| GERD / acid reflux | Heartburn, sour regurgitation, symptoms after meals or lying down | Dysphagia, gastrointestinal bleeding, unexplained weight loss or persistent symptoms |\n| Gastritis / peptic ulcer | Upper-abdominal discomfort, nausea, possible NSAID/H. pylori context | Anaemia, vomiting blood, black stool, progressive weight loss |\n| Gallstones / biliary disease | Episodic right upper-abdominal pain, sometimes after fatty food | Fever, jaundice, severe pain or dark urine |\n| Acute pancreatitis | Often severe upper-abdominal pain with back radiation and vomiting | Acute severe illness requires urgent hospital testing and imaging |\n| Pancreatic malignancy | Possible progressive pain, weight loss, loss of appetite, jaundice or new diabetes | Combined red flags require prompt imaging assessment, not a trial of indefinite antacids |\n\n## Why response to antacids is not definitive\n\nPartial relief can occur in several conditions; lack of relief also occurs in benign problems. A doctor considers medical history, abdominal examination, alarm features and investigations, rather than relying on response to acid-suppressing drugs alone.\n\n## When H. pylori testing is useful\n\nStool antigen or breath testing may be appropriate for selected patients with uncomplicated dyspepsia. Proton-pump inhibitors, bismuth and antibiotics can affect test accuracy; clinicians should provide the correct washout advice. New jaundice and substantial weight loss require a different workup and should not be delayed by H. pylori testing.\n\n## Where QXL can help\n\nQXL lists H. pylori stool antigen, CBC and liver/pancreatic biochemistry. The clinician selects tests based on presentation. The lab should never sell an expansive tumour-marker panel as routine first-line care for heartburn.\n\n**Related:** [pancreatic cancer versus acidity](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-vs-acidity), [warning signs](/blog/pancreatic-cancer-early-warning-signs).\n\n## References\n\n- [ACG dyspepsia](https://gi.org/guideline/management-of-dyspepsia/)\n- [NICE NG12](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng12/chapter/recommendations-organised-by-site-of-cancer)\n\n## QXL Diagnostics: test availability, TAT and enquiries\n\n- **Test availability:** Test available at QXL Diagnostics. Confirm the individual requested test when enquiring.\n- **Turnaround time (TAT):** Same Day (indicative; individual tests and sample cut-offs must be confirmed).\n- **Test details and unconfirmed information:** Contact QXL Diagnostics Super Speciality Lab Bengaluru.\n- **WhatsApp for Details:** [Message QXL Diagnostics](https://wa.me/919964639639?text=Hello%20QXL%20Diagnostics%2C%20please%20share%20the%20test%20details%2C%20sample%20requirements%2C%20current%20price%20and%20confirmed%20turnaround%20time%20for%20pancreatic%20or%20gastrointestinal%20laboratory%20tests.).\n- **Phone:** [+91 99646 39639](tel:+919964639639).\n\n**Please note:** Same Day is an indicative laboratory turnaround, subject to sample receipt/cut-off, individual assay workflow, and confirmation by QXL. Some specialised and stool tests may take longer. Urgent symptoms require clinical care, not waiting for a result. This service is for clinician-directed laboratory assessment. No laboratory package screens for, diagnoses, or rules out pancreatic cancer.\n\n## Medical sources and evidence\n\n- [NCI symptoms](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/symptoms)\n- [NCI diagnosis](https://www.cancer.gov/types/pancreatic/diagnosis)\n\n\n## Questions patients frequently ask\n\n### Does pancreatic pain always radiate to the back?\n\nNo. Back radiation may occur but is not required and is not specific for cancer.\n\n### Can H. pylori cause indigestion?\n\nYes. It can be associated with gastritis and ulcers; testing should follow a clinician-selected dyspepsia pathway.\n\n### Should persistent acidity always trigger CA 19-9?\n\nNo. CA 19-9 is not suitable as a routine dyspepsia screening test.\n",
