    "excerpt": "Quick answer: AMH is a hormone produced mainly by granulosa cells of small ovarian follicles. In fertility care it is used primarily as a marker of ovarian reserve and to help estimate ovarian response to stimulation. It does not measure egg quality and a single AMH result cannot tell a woman whethe",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** AMH is a hormone produced mainly by granulosa cells of small ovarian follicles. In fertility care it is used primarily as a marker of ovarian reserve and to help estimate ovarian response to stimulation. It does not measure egg quality and a single AMH result cannot tell a woman whether she will or will not conceive naturally.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Fertility and ovarian reserve\n\n## What is the AMH (Anti-Mullerian Hormone) test?\n\nAMH is a hormone produced mainly by granulosa cells of small ovarian follicles. In fertility care it is used primarily as a marker of ovarian reserve and to help estimate ovarian response to stimulation. It does not measure egg quality and a single AMH result cannot tell a woman whether she will or will not conceive naturally. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Assessment of ovarian reserve during infertility evaluation\n- Planning ovarian stimulation for IVF or egg freezing\n- Estimating risk of low or excessive ovarian response\n- Assessment before gonadotoxic chemotherapy or ovarian surgery in selected patients\n- Supporting evaluation of PCOS when interpreted with clinical and ultrasound findings, not as a stand-alone diagnosis\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nUsually a blood sample; fasting is generally not required. AMH is less cycle-dependent than FSH, but results should still be interpreted using the assay-specific reference interval and the patient’s age. Hormonal contraception, recent pregnancy, ovarian surgery and some treatments may influence values.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nLower AMH generally suggests a smaller recruitable follicle pool and may predict fewer oocytes obtained during stimulation. Higher AMH often reflects a larger follicle pool and may occur in PCOS; it can also indicate higher risk of ovarian hyper-response during stimulation. AMH should not be converted into a fixed “years of fertility left” estimate. Age remains a major determinant of oocyte quality and reproductive outcome.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Different AMH assays are not perfectly interchangeable.\n- AMH is not a direct egg-count or egg-quality test.\n- A low AMH does not mean natural conception is impossible; a high AMH does not guarantee fertility.\n- Population screening of women without infertility should not be used to predict time to pregnancy from a single AMH result.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- FSH, LH and estradiol when clinically indicated\n- Antral follicle count by ultrasound\n- TSH and prolactin in infertility work-up\n- Androgen profile when PCOS or hyperandrogenism is suspected\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Can AMH be tested on any day of the menstrual cycle?\n\nIn most situations yes, because AMH varies less across the cycle than FSH. However, the result still needs age- and assay-specific interpretation.\n\n### Does low AMH mean infertility?\n\nNo. It suggests reduced ovarian reserve and may predict a lower response to ovarian stimulation, but it does not by itself determine whether natural pregnancy can occur.\n\n### Does high AMH mean PCOS?\n\nNot by itself. High AMH can be seen in PCOS, but PCOS is diagnosed using clinical, biochemical and/or ultrasound criteria rather than AMH alone.\n\n### Can supplements increase AMH?\n\nA change in a laboratory value does not necessarily mean the ovarian follicle pool has increased. Patients should be cautious about products marketed as “AMH boosters.”\n\n### Is AMH an egg-quality test?\n\nNo. Egg quality is strongly related to age and cannot be measured by AMH.\n\n### When is AMH most useful?\n\nIt is most useful in fertility treatment planning, especially for estimating ovarian response to stimulation.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for AMH: check with QXL for more information. See the [AMH (Anti-Mullerian Hormone) test booking](/amh-test), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [ACOG - Use of Anti-Mullerian Hormone in Women Not Seeking Fertility Care](https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2019/04/the-use-of-antimullerian-hormone-in-women-not-seeking-fertility-care)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
