    "excerpt": "Quick answer: Cortisol is the main glucocorticoid produced by the adrenal cortex. It follows a strong daily rhythm and rises with physical or psychological stress. Because timing matters, a random cortisol value can be difficult to interpret; the correct test depends on whether the concern is cortis",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Cortisol is the main glucocorticoid produced by the adrenal cortex. It follows a strong daily rhythm and rises with physical or psychological stress. Because timing matters, a random cortisol value can be difficult to interpret; the correct test depends on whether the concern is cortisol deficiency or cortisol excess.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Adrenal and pituitary function\n\n## What is the Cortisol test?\n\nCortisol is the main glucocorticoid produced by the adrenal cortex. It follows a strong daily rhythm and rises with physical or psychological stress. Because timing matters, a random cortisol value can be difficult to interpret; the correct test depends on whether the concern is cortisol deficiency or cortisol excess. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Suspected adrenal insufficiency or adrenal crisis\n- Investigation of possible Cushing syndrome\n- Assessment of pituitary-adrenal disorders\n- Monitoring selected endocrine treatment protocols\n- Dynamic testing such as ACTH stimulation or dexamethasone suppression when ordered by a clinician\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nThe requested sampling time should be followed exactly. Morning serum cortisol is commonly used when adrenal insufficiency is suspected. For cortisol excess, clinicians may use late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour urinary free cortisol or dexamethasone suppression testing. Acute illness, pain, sleep disruption, pregnancy, estrogen therapy and steroid medicines can alter interpretation.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nA low morning cortisol can raise concern for adrenal insufficiency, especially when symptoms and ACTH are concordant; an intermediate result often requires dynamic testing. A high serum cortisol by itself does not diagnose Cushing syndrome because stress and increased cortisol-binding globulin can elevate total cortisol. Loss of normal circadian suppression or failure to suppress during a validated dynamic test is more informative for cortisol excess.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Total cortisol is influenced by cortisol-binding globulin.\n- Exogenous glucocorticoids may suppress endogenous cortisol and some can interfere analytically.\n- Acute illness and stress can markedly raise cortisol.\n- The diagnostic threshold depends on sampling time, assay and dynamic-test protocol.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- ACTH\n- Electrolytes and glucose\n- ACTH stimulation test\n- Dexamethasone suppression test\n- 24-hour urinary free cortisol or late-night salivary cortisol\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nSevere weakness with vomiting, dehydration, confusion, collapse or low blood pressure can be compatible with adrenal crisis and requires emergency treatment. Do not wait for outpatient test interpretation.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why does the time of day matter?\n\nCortisol normally peaks around waking and falls toward night. The same number can have different meaning depending on collection time.\n\n### Can stress increase cortisol?\n\nYes. Pain, anxiety, infection, surgery, poor sleep and intense exercise can increase cortisol.\n\n### Can steroid medicines affect the result?\n\nYes. Oral, injected, inhaled, topical and nasal glucocorticoids may affect the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis; the clinician and laboratory should know about them.\n\n### Does a high cortisol result prove Cushing syndrome?\n\nNo. Cushing syndrome requires appropriate screening and confirmation in clinical context.\n\n### What symptoms can occur with low cortisol?\n\nFatigue, weight loss, nausea, low blood pressure, dizziness and salt craving can occur, but these symptoms are nonspecific.\n\n### When is low cortisol an emergency?\n\nSevere weakness with vomiting, dehydration, confusion or low blood pressure can indicate adrenal crisis and needs immediate emergency care.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for Cortisol: check with QXL for more information. See the [Cortisol test booking](/cortisol-test), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [Endocrine Society - Primary Adrenal Insufficiency guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/primary-adrenal-insufficiency)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
