    "excerpt": "Quick answer: Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is produced by prostate tissue. Total PSA is the usual first blood marker used in prostate-cancer risk assessment and follow-up. Free PSA is the unbound fraction of circulating PSA; the free-to-total PSA percentage can sometimes help refine cancer risk w",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) is produced by prostate tissue. Total PSA is the usual first blood marker used in prostate-cancer risk assessment and follow-up. Free PSA is the unbound fraction of circulating PSA; the free-to-total PSA percentage can sometimes help refine cancer risk when total PSA is in an intermediate range. PSA is prostate-specific, not cancer-specific.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Prostate health\n\n## What is the PSA Total + Free PSA test?\n\nProstate-specific antigen (PSA) is produced by prostate tissue. Total PSA is the usual first blood marker used in prostate-cancer risk assessment and follow-up. Free PSA is the unbound fraction of circulating PSA; the free-to-total PSA percentage can sometimes help refine cancer risk when total PSA is in an intermediate range. PSA is prostate-specific, not cancer-specific. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Shared decision-making for prostate cancer screening in appropriate men\n- Evaluation of an elevated or rising PSA\n- Supporting decisions about further urological assessment\n- Monitoring after treatment for prostate cancer\n- Distinguishing benign from malignant causes in selected patients when free PSA adds information\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nA blood sample is used. PSA can be affected by benign prostatic enlargement, prostatitis, urinary retention, recent prostate manipulation and some medicines such as 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors. Clinicians may repeat a newly elevated PSA before moving to imaging, biomarkers or biopsy. Follow local instructions about recent ejaculation, cycling or urological procedures.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nHigher total PSA is associated with increasing probability of clinically significant prostate disease, but no single value confirms cancer. A lower percentage of free PSA, in an appropriate clinical setting, is generally associated with a higher probability of prostate cancer than a higher percentage. Age, prostate volume, PSA trend, family history, ancestry, examination, MRI and other risk tools may all influence next steps.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- PSA can be elevated in benign prostatic hyperplasia and inflammation.\n- A normal PSA does not completely exclude prostate cancer.\n- Free PSA should be interpreted only with total PSA and the laboratory method.\n- Screening decisions should use shared decision-making rather than an automatic annual test for everyone.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Digital rectal examination when clinically appropriate\n- Urinalysis/culture if infection suspected\n- Prostate MRI and urological risk assessment\n- Biopsy only when clinically indicated\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does a high PSA mean prostate cancer?\n\nNo. Benign enlargement, inflammation, urinary retention and recent prostate procedures can also raise PSA.\n\n### Why measure free PSA?\n\nIn selected men, the proportion of PSA that is free can improve risk estimation when total PSA is not clearly low or clearly high.\n\n### Should an elevated PSA be repeated?\n\nOften yes. Current urological guidance supports confirming a newly elevated PSA before proceeding to secondary biomarkers, imaging or biopsy when clinically safe.\n\n### Can finasteride or dutasteride affect PSA?\n\nYes. These medicines can lower PSA substantially and require adjusted clinical interpretation.\n\n### Is PSA a screening test or diagnostic test?\n\nIt is a risk-assessment and monitoring biomarker. Cancer diagnosis ultimately requires clinical assessment and, when indicated, tissue diagnosis.\n\n### What should I do if PSA is high?\n\nDiscuss the result with a doctor or urologist rather than assuming cancer. The next step depends on age, symptoms, prior PSA values, medicines and overall risk.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for PSA Total + Free PSA: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [AUA/SUO - Early Detection of Prostate Cancer guideline](https://www.auanet.org/guidelines-and-quality/guidelines/early-detection-of-prostate-cancer-guidelines)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
