    "excerpt": "Quick answer: DHEA-S is an androgen produced predominantly by the adrenal glands. Because it is relatively stable in blood and much less influenced by short-term fluctuations than unconjugated DHEA, it is useful when evaluating adrenal androgen excess or deficiency.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** DHEA-S is an androgen produced predominantly by the adrenal glands. Because it is relatively stable in blood and much less influenced by short-term fluctuations than unconjugated DHEA, it is useful when evaluating adrenal androgen excess or deficiency.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Adrenal androgen evaluation\n\n## What is the DHEA-S (Dehydroepiandrosterone Sulfate) test?\n\nDHEA-S is an androgen produced predominantly by the adrenal glands. Because it is relatively stable in blood and much less influenced by short-term fluctuations than unconjugated DHEA, it is useful when evaluating adrenal androgen excess or deficiency. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Hirsutism, acne or virilisation\n- Evaluation of androgen excess in women\n- Assessment of possible adrenal source of elevated androgens\n- Selected evaluation of precocious or delayed puberty\n- Assessment of adrenal function in specific endocrine disorders\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nUsually no fasting is required, but local instructions should be followed. Interpretation depends strongly on age and sex because DHEA-S is high in young adulthood and falls with age. Inform the clinician about DHEA supplements, glucocorticoids, hormonal therapy and other medicines.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nMarked elevation may suggest adrenal androgen overproduction and can occur in congenital adrenal hyperplasia or, less commonly, an androgen-secreting adrenal tumour. Mild to moderate elevation is more common in conditions such as PCOS. Low values can occur with adrenal insufficiency or with age, but DHEA-S is not a stand-alone diagnostic test for adrenal failure.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Age-specific reference intervals are essential.\n- DHEA supplements can make interpretation misleading.\n- DHEA-S helps localise androgen source but does not by itself establish a diagnosis.\n- Results should be considered with testosterone, 17-OHP and clinical signs.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Total and free testosterone with SHBG\n- 17-hydroxyprogesterone\n- Cortisol and ACTH when adrenal disease is suspected\n- Pelvic or adrenal imaging only when clinically indicated\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why is DHEA-S useful in women with excess facial hair?\n\nIt helps assess whether excess androgen production may be coming from the adrenal glands.\n\n### Is DHEA-S the same as testosterone?\n\nNo. Both are androgens, but DHEA-S is mainly adrenal in origin while testosterone can arise from the ovaries, testes and peripheral conversion.\n\n### Can PCOS raise DHEA-S?\n\nYes, mild or moderate elevation can occur in PCOS, but very high values may prompt evaluation for other causes.\n\n### Does low DHEA-S prove adrenal insufficiency?\n\nNo. It may support the picture but cortisol/ACTH testing and clinical assessment are more important.\n\n### Does age affect DHEA-S?\n\nStrongly. Levels typically decline with age, so age-specific ranges are necessary.\n\n### Should I stop DHEA supplements before testing?\n\nTell your clinician and laboratory about any DHEA supplement. Do not stop prescribed therapy without medical advice.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for DHEA-S: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [Endocrine Society - Hirsutism guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/hirsutism)\n- [Endocrine Society - Primary Adrenal Insufficiency guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/primary-adrenal-insufficiency)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
