    "id": "qxl-pack-21",
    "title": "Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG Test: Uses, Preparation, Results, Limitations & FAQs",
    "seo_title": "Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG Test Guide | QXL Bengaluru",
    "meta_description": "Patient guide to the Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG test: what it measures, why doctors order it, preparation, how results are interpreted, limitations and FAQs. QXL Bengaluru.",
    "canonical_url": "https://qxldiagnostics.com/blog/testosterone-shbg-profile-patient-guide",
    "slug": "testosterone-shbg-profile-patient-guide",
    "category": "Hormones & Endocrinology",
    "reviewer": "Dr. Shantakumar Muruda",
    "reviewer_title": "MD (Medical Biochemistry), Senior Consultant, Founder & CEO, QXL Diagnostics",
    "review_pending": false,
    "keywords": [
      "Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG test",
      "Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG test Bengaluru",
      "Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG test Bangalore",
      "Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG results meaning",
      "Androgen and gonadal function"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Quick answer: Total testosterone measures bound plus unbound testosterone. SHBG is the principal high-affinity binding protein. Free or calculated free testosterone can add clinically useful information when SHBG is unusually high or low, or when symptoms and total testosterone do not agree.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Total testosterone measures bound plus unbound testosterone. SHBG is the principal high-affinity binding protein. Free or calculated free testosterone can add clinically useful information when SHBG is unusually high or low, or when symptoms and total testosterone do not agree.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Androgen and gonadal function\n\n## What is the Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG test?\n\nTotal testosterone measures bound plus unbound testosterone. SHBG is the principal high-affinity binding protein. Free or calculated free testosterone can add clinically useful information when SHBG is unusually high or low, or when symptoms and total testosterone do not agree. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Symptoms of male hypogonadism such as reduced libido, erectile dysfunction, loss of body hair or unexplained low bone density\n- Evaluation of hirsutism, acne, menstrual disturbance or virilisation in women\n- Assessment of altered SHBG due to obesity, thyroid disease, liver disease, medicines or estrogen exposure\n- Monitoring selected endocrine therapy\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nFor evaluation of male hypogonadism, testosterone is generally measured in the morning and a low result should usually be confirmed on a separate day. Acute illness, poor sleep, calorie restriction and some medicines can lower testosterone. The laboratory method matters, especially at low concentrations seen in women and children.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nA diagnosis of male hypogonadism requires compatible symptoms/signs plus consistently low testosterone; a single low value is insufficient. Low SHBG can make total testosterone appear low while free testosterone is relatively preserved, as may occur with obesity or insulin resistance. High SHBG can increase total testosterone while free testosterone may be low. In women, clearly elevated testosterone can support biochemical hyperandrogenism and may guide evaluation for PCOS or rarer androgen-secreting disorders.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Free testosterone assays vary in quality; calculated free testosterone may be preferred when reliable total testosterone and SHBG are available.\n- Acute illness can transiently lower testosterone.\n- Biotin and other assay-specific interferences may affect some immunoassays.\n- Interpretation is sex-, age- and method-specific.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- LH and FSH\n- Prolactin\n- DHEA-S and 17-OHP\n- Estradiol where appropriate\n- TSH and liver tests when SHBG alteration is suspected\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why do I need SHBG with testosterone?\n\nSHBG affects how much testosterone is bound. Measuring it helps explain why total testosterone may not match symptoms.\n\n### Should testosterone be measured in the morning?\n\nFor men being evaluated for hypogonadism, morning testing is generally recommended because of diurnal variation.\n\n### Can obesity lower total testosterone?\n\nYes. Obesity can lower SHBG and therefore lower total testosterone; free testosterone may not fall to the same degree.\n\n### Can birth-control pills affect SHBG?\n\nEstrogen-containing preparations can raise SHBG and alter total/free testosterone relationships.\n\n### Does one low testosterone result diagnose hypogonadism?\n\nNo. Symptoms plus consistently low concentrations are required, usually confirmed with repeat testing.\n\n### Why are testosterone tests harder to interpret in women?\n\nFemale concentrations are much lower, so assay sensitivity and specificity become especially important.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for Free Testosterone / Total Testosterone + SHBG: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [Endocrine Society - Testosterone Therapy for Hypogonadism guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/testosterone-therapy)\n- [Endocrine Society - Hirsutism guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/hirsutism)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
