    "excerpt": "Quick answer: 17-hydroxyprogesterone is a steroid precursor in cortisol synthesis. Its main diagnostic use is screening for 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the most common form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), and evaluating selected patients with androgen excess.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** 17-hydroxyprogesterone is a steroid precursor in cortisol synthesis. Its main diagnostic use is screening for 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the most common form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), and evaluating selected patients with androgen excess.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Congenital adrenal hyperplasia and androgen excess\n\n## What is the 17-OH Progesterone (17-OHP) test?\n\n17-hydroxyprogesterone is a steroid precursor in cortisol synthesis. Its main diagnostic use is screening for 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the most common form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH), and evaluating selected patients with androgen excess. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Newborn screening or evaluation for congenital adrenal hyperplasia\n- Women with hyperandrogenism or hirsutism when non-classic CAH is a possibility\n- Children with early virilisation or precocious pubertal signs\n- Evaluation of adrenal steroid pathways\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nFor evaluation of non-classic CAH in menstruating women, an early-morning sample in the follicular phase is often preferred. Time of day and menstrual phase can matter. Markedly abnormal or borderline results may require an ACTH stimulation test. Steroid medicines can alter results.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nMarkedly elevated 17-OHP can indicate 21-hydroxylase deficiency. Borderline basal results are not diagnostic and may require ACTH-stimulated measurement. Mild elevations may occur because of physiologic variation, stress or assay cross-reactivity, especially with some immunoassays.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Newborns and premature infants require age- and gestational-age-specific interpretation.\n- Immunoassay cross-reactivity can cause false elevation; LC-MS/MS can improve specificity in selected settings.\n- A borderline value should not be labelled CAH without appropriate confirmation.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- ACTH stimulation test\n- Cortisol and ACTH\n- DHEA-S, androstenedione and testosterone\n- Electrolytes in classic salt-wasting CAH\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What condition is 17-OHP mainly used to detect?\n\nIt is primarily used to screen for 21-hydroxylase deficiency, including classic and non-classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia.\n\n### Why is early-morning testing used?\n\nAdrenal steroid secretion has diurnal variation; standardised timing improves interpretation.\n\n### Can PCOS and non-classic CAH look similar?\n\nYes. Both can cause hirsutism and irregular periods, which is why 17-OHP may be included in the evaluation.\n\n### Does a borderline 17-OHP mean CAH?\n\nNo. Borderline results often need ACTH stimulation and specialist interpretation.\n\n### Can pregnancy or cycle phase affect the result?\n\nHormonal state can influence steroid concentrations, so clinical context and timing matter.\n\n### Can the assay method matter?\n\nYes. Cross-reactivity differs among immunoassays, and mass-spectrometry methods may offer greater specificity.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for 17-OH Progesterone: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [Endocrine Society - Hirsutism guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/hirsutism)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
