    "excerpt": "Quick answer: Anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies are autoantibodies strongly associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). They are most useful when interpreted with symptoms, ANA testing, other autoantibodies, complement levels and urinalysis rather than as a stand-alone screening test in pe",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies are autoantibodies strongly associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). They are most useful when interpreted with symptoms, ANA testing, other autoantibodies, complement levels and urinalysis rather than as a stand-alone screening test in people with nonspecific symptoms.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Autoimmune and lupus assessment\n\n## What is the Anti-dsDNA Antibody test?\n\nAnti-double-stranded DNA antibodies are autoantibodies strongly associated with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). They are most useful when interpreted with symptoms, ANA testing, other autoantibodies, complement levels and urinalysis rather than as a stand-alone screening test in people with nonspecific symptoms. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Supporting diagnosis of SLE in a patient with compatible clinical features\n- Assessing suspected lupus nephritis with other kidney tests\n- Monitoring disease activity in selected patients when the patient’s anti-dsDNA level tracks with disease activity\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nA blood sample is required; fasting is generally not necessary. The laboratory method should be considered because ELISA/EIA, Crithidia luciliae immunofluorescence and other techniques differ in sensitivity, specificity and avidity detection.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nA clearly positive anti-dsDNA in the appropriate clinical setting supports SLE, but a negative result does not exclude lupus. Rising titres can accompany disease activity in some patients, especially renal disease, but the relationship is not perfect. Results should be correlated with C3/C4, urine protein/albumin, serum creatinine and clinical findings.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Different anti-dsDNA methods can give different results.\n- Low-level positivity can occur outside SLE depending on assay.\n- A result should not be interpreted without ANA and clinical context.\n- Not every patient with SLE has anti-dsDNA antibodies.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- ANA by HEp-2 IIF\n- ENA profile\n- Complement C3 and C4\n- Urinalysis, urine protein/creatinine ratio and creatinine\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Is anti-dsDNA specific for lupus?\n\nIt is one of the more SLE-associated autoantibodies, but diagnosis still requires clinical correlation.\n\n### Can anti-dsDNA be negative in lupus?\n\nYes. A negative result does not rule out SLE.\n\n### Does a higher result mean more severe disease?\n\nNot always. In some patients levels track activity, particularly nephritis, while in others they do not.\n\n### Why test C3 and C4 with anti-dsDNA?\n\nComplement consumption can accompany active immune-complex disease and adds context to anti-dsDNA changes.\n\n### Do I need fasting?\n\nUsually no.\n\n### Why can two labs report different anti-dsDNA results?\n\nAssays detect antibodies differently, so results and units may not be interchangeable.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for Anti-dsDNA Antibody: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
