    "excerpt": "Quick answer: An extractable nuclear antigen (ENA) profile measures a group of specific autoantibodies—commonly including SSA/Ro, SSB/La, Sm, U1-RNP and sometimes Scl-70, Jo-1 and others depending on the panel. The pattern can help clinicians classify autoimmune connective-tissue diseases after an a",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** An extractable nuclear antigen (ENA) profile measures a group of specific autoantibodies—commonly including SSA/Ro, SSB/La, Sm, U1-RNP and sometimes Scl-70, Jo-1 and others depending on the panel. The pattern can help clinicians classify autoimmune connective-tissue diseases after an appropriate clinical assessment.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Autoimmune connective-tissue disease assessment\n\n## What is the ENA Profile test?\n\nAn extractable nuclear antigen (ENA) profile measures a group of specific autoantibodies—commonly including SSA/Ro, SSB/La, Sm, U1-RNP and sometimes Scl-70, Jo-1 and others depending on the panel. The pattern can help clinicians classify autoimmune connective-tissue diseases after an appropriate clinical assessment. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Further evaluation after a positive ANA when symptoms suggest connective-tissue disease\n- Suspected SLE, Sjogren syndrome, systemic sclerosis, mixed connective-tissue disease or inflammatory myositis\n- Pregnancy risk assessment in women with anti-SSA/Ro or anti-SSB/La where clinically relevant\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nBlood sample; fasting is usually not required. The exact antigens included in an ENA panel vary by laboratory, so the report should be read antigen by antigen rather than simply as “ENA positive.”\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nAnti-Sm is strongly associated with SLE; anti-U1-RNP is associated with mixed connective-tissue disease and can occur in SLE; anti-SSA/Ro and anti-SSB/La are associated with Sjogren syndrome and can occur in SLE; other panel antibodies may point toward systemic sclerosis or inflammatory myositis. None should be interpreted in isolation from symptoms and ANA pattern/titre.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Different laboratories include different ENA antigens.\n- Autoantibodies can precede symptoms or occur at low levels without a defined systemic disease.\n- A negative ENA profile does not exclude autoimmune disease.\n- The ANA pattern and clinical phenotype remain important.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- ANA HEp-2 IIF\n- Anti-dsDNA\n- C3/C4\n- Urinalysis and organ-specific tests guided by symptoms\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What does ENA stand for?\n\nExtractable nuclear antigens, a group of cellular targets recognised by specific autoantibodies.\n\n### Is ENA the same as ANA?\n\nNo. ANA is a broader screening test; ENA identifies specific nuclear-antigen antibodies.\n\n### Can ENA be positive with a negative ANA?\n\nIt can occur depending on assay and antibody type, but discordant results should be reviewed carefully.\n\n### Does anti-SSA/Ro always mean Sjogren syndrome?\n\nNo. It can occur in Sjogren syndrome, lupus and other settings.\n\n### What is anti-Sm associated with?\n\nAnti-Sm is strongly associated with systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\n### Should ENA be used as a general wellness screen?\n\nNo. It is most useful when there is a clinical reason to suspect autoimmune connective-tissue disease.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for ENA Profile: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
