    "excerpt": "Quick answer: ANCA testing evaluates autoantibodies associated with small-vessel vasculitis. Modern evaluation commonly combines antigen-specific antibodies to proteinase 3 (PR3) and myeloperoxidase (MPO), sometimes with indirect immunofluorescence. A positive result supports but does not by itself ",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** ANCA testing evaluates autoantibodies associated with small-vessel vasculitis. Modern evaluation commonly combines antigen-specific antibodies to proteinase 3 (PR3) and myeloperoxidase (MPO), sometimes with indirect immunofluorescence. A positive result supports but does not by itself diagnose ANCA-associated vasculitis.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Vasculitis testing\n\n## What is the ANCA (PR3 / MPO) test?\n\nANCA testing evaluates autoantibodies associated with small-vessel vasculitis. Modern evaluation commonly combines antigen-specific antibodies to proteinase 3 (PR3) and myeloperoxidase (MPO), sometimes with indirect immunofluorescence. A positive result supports but does not by itself diagnose ANCA-associated vasculitis. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Suspected granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis or eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis\n- Unexplained rapidly progressive kidney injury with haematuria/proteinuria\n- Pulmonary-renal syndrome, recurrent sinus disease with systemic features, vasculitic neuropathy or other compatible manifestations\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nBlood sample; fasting is generally not required. ANCA should be ordered because of a compatible clinical picture, not as a broad screen for fatigue or nonspecific pain.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nPR3-ANCA is more often associated with granulomatosis with polyangiitis, while MPO-ANCA is more often associated with microscopic polyangiitis, but there is overlap. ANCA positivity may occur in inflammatory bowel disease, autoimmune liver disease, infection, drug exposure and other conditions. Diagnosis of vasculitis depends on clinical features, imaging, urine/kidney tests and sometimes biopsy.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- ANCA positivity is not synonymous with vasculitis.\n- Some true cases of ANCA-associated vasculitis can be ANCA-negative.\n- Antigen-specific PR3/MPO results and immunofluorescence patterns should be interpreted together when available.\n- Serial ANCA values alone should not determine treatment changes.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Urinalysis and urine protein assessment\n- Serum creatinine/eGFR\n- CBC, CRP/ESR\n- Chest imaging and tissue biopsy when indicated\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What are PR3 and MPO?\n\nThey are neutrophil enzymes that can be targets of ANCA autoantibodies.\n\n### Does a positive ANCA prove vasculitis?\n\nNo. It raises or lowers probability depending on the clinical picture.\n\n### Can infection cause ANCA positivity?\n\nYes, some infections and other inflammatory conditions can produce positive ANCA results.\n\n### What symptoms make ANCA testing more useful?\n\nKidney inflammation, pulmonary haemorrhage, persistent destructive sinus disease, systemic vasculitic features and certain neuropathies are examples.\n\n### Can vasculitis occur with a negative ANCA?\n\nYes.\n\n### Is ANCA a routine screening test?\n\nNo. Testing performs best when the pre-test clinical probability is appropriate.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for ANCA: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
