    "excerpt": "Quick answer: C3 and C4 are complement proteins involved in immune defence and immune-complex clearance. They are commonly measured in systemic lupus erythematosus and other immune-complex disorders to look for complement consumption, but values are not specific to one disease.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** C3 and C4 are complement proteins involved in immune defence and immune-complex clearance. They are commonly measured in systemic lupus erythematosus and other immune-complex disorders to look for complement consumption, but values are not specific to one disease.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Autoimmune and complement assessment\n\n## What is the Complement C3 & C4 test?\n\nC3 and C4 are complement proteins involved in immune defence and immune-complex clearance. They are commonly measured in systemic lupus erythematosus and other immune-complex disorders to look for complement consumption, but values are not specific to one disease. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Assessing suspected or known SLE\n- Monitoring selected patients with lupus nephritis or immune-complex disease\n- Evaluation of recurrent infections or suspected complement deficiency in specialist settings\n- Investigation of certain kidney disorders such as glomerulonephritis\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nBlood sample; fasting is usually unnecessary. Acute inflammation can increase complement proteins, while immune-complex activation can consume them. Laboratory method and reference range matter.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nLow C3 and/or C4 may reflect complement consumption, reduced synthesis or inherited deficiency. In active SLE, both may be reduced, but patterns differ across diseases. Normal C3/C4 do not exclude autoimmune disease. High values usually reflect an acute-phase response and are less diagnostically specific.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Complement levels can be influenced by inflammation, liver synthesis and inherited variation.\n- The same patient should ideally be followed with the same laboratory method when trends matter.\n- Functional complement tests such as CH50/AH50 may be needed in suspected inherited deficiency.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- ANA, anti-dsDNA and ENA profile\n- CH50/AH50 when indicated\n- Urinalysis, creatinine and urine protein\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why are C3 and C4 low in lupus?\n\nImmune complexes can activate and consume complement proteins during active disease.\n\n### Do normal C3 and C4 rule out lupus?\n\nNo.\n\n### Can C3 and C4 be high?\n\nYes. They can rise as acute-phase proteins during inflammation.\n\n### What if only C4 is low?\n\nThe pattern can have several explanations, including classical-pathway activation or inherited low C4; clinical context is needed.\n\n### Are C3 and C4 disease-specific?\n\nNo. They are supportive biomarkers, not disease-specific diagnostic markers.\n\n### Do I need fasting?\n\nUsually not.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for Complement C3 & C4: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
