    "excerpt": "Quick answer: CA-125 is a blood tumour marker most closely associated with epithelial ovarian cancer, but it is not cancer-specific. It can also rise in benign gynecologic conditions, menstruation, pregnancy and inflammation of the peritoneum or pleura. It is mainly useful in defined diagnostic and ",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** CA-125 is a blood tumour marker most closely associated with epithelial ovarian cancer, but it is not cancer-specific. It can also rise in benign gynecologic conditions, menstruation, pregnancy and inflammation of the peritoneum or pleura. It is mainly useful in defined diagnostic and monitoring pathways, not as a stand-alone screening test for average-risk women.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Ovarian and gynecologic tumour-marker monitoring\n\n## What is the CA-125 test?\n\nCA-125 is a blood tumour marker most closely associated with epithelial ovarian cancer, but it is not cancer-specific. It can also rise in benign gynecologic conditions, menstruation, pregnancy and inflammation of the peritoneum or pleura. It is mainly useful in defined diagnostic and monitoring pathways, not as a stand-alone screening test for average-risk women. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Evaluation of an adnexal/pelvic mass with imaging and clinical risk assessment\n- Monitoring response to treatment in known ovarian cancer\n- Monitoring for recurrence in selected patients\n- Use in validated risk algorithms when clinically appropriate\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nBlood sample; fasting is usually not required. Menstrual status, pregnancy and known benign gynecologic conditions should be considered during interpretation.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nA raised CA-125 does not diagnose ovarian cancer. Very high or rising levels can increase concern in the right clinical context, but imaging and specialist assessment are essential. In a patient with known ovarian cancer, trends measured using the same assay can be more informative than a single value.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Not recommended as a general stand-alone ovarian cancer screening test for average-risk asymptomatic women.\n- Benign endometriosis, fibroids, menstruation, pregnancy and serosal inflammation can raise CA-125.\n- Some ovarian cancers do not produce much CA-125.\n- Assay trends should preferably be followed on the same platform.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Pelvic/transvaginal ultrasound\n- HE4 and ROMA in selected settings\n- Other tumour markers based on age and mass characteristics\n- Gynecologic oncology assessment when indicated\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does high CA-125 mean ovarian cancer?\n\nNo. Many benign conditions can raise CA-125.\n\n### Can CA-125 be normal in ovarian cancer?\n\nYes, especially in some early-stage or non-CA-125-producing tumours.\n\n### Should every woman have CA-125 screening?\n\nNo. It has not been shown to be an effective stand-alone screening test for average-risk asymptomatic women.\n\n### Can endometriosis raise CA-125?\n\nYes.\n\n### Why is CA-125 useful after ovarian cancer treatment?\n\nSerial trends can help monitor response and detect biochemical recurrence in appropriate patients.\n\n### Is fasting required?\n\nUsually no.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for CA-125: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [National Cancer Institute - Cancer Screening Tests](https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/screening/screening-tests)\n- [National Cancer Institute - Tumor Marker Tests in Common Use](https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/diagnosis-staging/diagnosis/tumor-markers-list)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
