    "excerpt": "Quick answer: CEA is a tumour-associated glycoprotein used mainly in the management of colorectal cancer and in selected other cancers. It is not suitable as a general cancer screening test because both false positives and false negatives occur.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** CEA is a tumour-associated glycoprotein used mainly in the management of colorectal cancer and in selected other cancers. It is not suitable as a general cancer screening test because both false positives and false negatives occur.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Cancer monitoring\n\n## What is the CEA (Carcinoembryonic Antigen) test?\n\nCEA is a tumour-associated glycoprotein used mainly in the management of colorectal cancer and in selected other cancers. It is not suitable as a general cancer screening test because both false positives and false negatives occur. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Baseline assessment in diagnosed colorectal cancer\n- Post-treatment surveillance in selected patients\n- Monitoring response to treatment\n- Use as an adjunct in selected gastrointestinal or other malignancies\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nBlood sample; fasting is usually not required. Smoking can raise CEA, and benign liver disease, inflammation and other conditions may cause elevation.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nIn a patient with known CEA-producing cancer, a sustained fall during treatment can support response and a confirmed rising trend may prompt evaluation for recurrence or progression. A single mildly elevated CEA in someone without known cancer is nonspecific and should not be treated as a cancer diagnosis.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Not a population screening test for colorectal or other cancer.\n- Smoking and benign disease can increase CEA.\n- Not all tumours produce CEA.\n- Serial changes are often more useful than one isolated value.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Colonoscopy and imaging based on clinical indication\n- Histopathology and molecular tumour testing in confirmed cancer\n- Liver function tests in selected contexts\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does high CEA mean colon cancer?\n\nNo. CEA can rise in smokers and several benign conditions, and other cancers can also raise it.\n\n### Can CEA be used for routine cancer screening?\n\nNo. It lacks sufficient accuracy for that purpose.\n\n### Why is CEA checked after colorectal cancer treatment?\n\nA rising trend can be one clue to recurrence in patients whose tumour produces CEA.\n\n### Does smoking affect CEA?\n\nYes, smokers can have higher baseline values.\n\n### Is one CEA value enough to judge treatment response?\n\nUsually not. Trends plus imaging and clinical findings are more informative.\n\n### Is fasting required?\n\nUsually no.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for CEA: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [National Cancer Institute - Tumor Marker Tests in Common Use](https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/diagnosis-staging/diagnosis/tumor-markers-list)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
