    "id": "qxl-pack-31",
    "title": "AFP (Alpha-Fetoprotein) Tumour Marker Test: Uses, Preparation, Results, Limitations & FAQs",
    "seo_title": "AFP Test: Uses, Results & FAQs | QXL Bengaluru",
    "meta_description": "Patient guide to the AFP (Alpha-Fetoprotein) Tumour Marker test: what it measures, why doctors order it, preparation, how results are interpreted, limitations and FAQs. QXL Bengaluru.",
    "canonical_url": "https://qxldiagnostics.com/blog/afp-alpha-fetoprotein-test-patient-guide",
    "slug": "afp-alpha-fetoprotein-test-patient-guide",
    "category": "Oncology & Pathology",
    "reviewer": "Dr. Shantakumar Muruda",
    "reviewer_title": "MD (Medical Biochemistry), Senior Consultant, Founder & CEO, QXL Diagnostics",
    "review_pending": false,
    "keywords": [
      "AFP test",
      "AFP test Bengaluru",
      "AFP test Bangalore",
      "AFP results meaning",
      "Liver and germ-cell tumour assessment"
    ],
    "excerpt": "Quick answer: AFP is a fetal protein that is normally very low in healthy nonpregnant adults. It can rise in hepatocellular carcinoma and certain germ-cell tumours, but it can also rise in pregnancy and active liver disease. It is therefore interpreted with imaging, clinical findings and other tumou",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** AFP is a fetal protein that is normally very low in healthy nonpregnant adults. It can rise in hepatocellular carcinoma and certain germ-cell tumours, but it can also rise in pregnancy and active liver disease. It is therefore interpreted with imaging, clinical findings and other tumour markers.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Liver and germ-cell tumour assessment\n\n## What is the AFP (Alpha-Fetoprotein) Tumour Marker test?\n\nAFP is a fetal protein that is normally very low in healthy nonpregnant adults. It can rise in hepatocellular carcinoma and certain germ-cell tumours, but it can also rise in pregnancy and active liver disease. It is therefore interpreted with imaging, clinical findings and other tumour markers. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Assessment and monitoring of hepatocellular carcinoma in appropriate high-risk pathways\n- Evaluation of suspected nonseminomatous germ-cell tumours\n- Staging, prognosis and treatment monitoring in AFP-producing germ-cell tumours\n- Follow-up after treatment when AFP was elevated at diagnosis\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nBlood sample; fasting is generally not required. Pregnancy status and active hepatitis/liver injury are important interpretive factors.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nA markedly elevated AFP can increase suspicion for hepatocellular carcinoma or an AFP-producing germ-cell tumour, but the pattern is not specific. In known AFP-producing cancer, the rate and direction of change can help assess treatment response. Normal AFP does not exclude liver cancer or germ-cell tumour.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- AFP can rise in active hepatitis and pregnancy.\n- Not all hepatocellular carcinomas or germ-cell tumours raise AFP.\n- Screening strategies for liver cancer depend on risk group and usually include imaging rather than AFP alone.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Liver ultrasound/CT/MRI when indicated\n- Liver function tests and viral hepatitis testing\n- beta-hCG and LDH for germ-cell tumour work-up\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Does high AFP always mean cancer?\n\nNo. Pregnancy and active liver disease can also raise AFP.\n\n### Can liver cancer occur with normal AFP?\n\nYes.\n\n### Why is AFP useful in testicular germ-cell tumours?\n\nIt can help classify, stage and monitor AFP-producing nonseminomatous germ-cell tumours.\n\n### Can AFP be used alone to screen everyone for liver cancer?\n\nNo. Surveillance is risk-based and typically relies heavily on imaging.\n\n### Does AFP fall after successful treatment?\n\nIt often falls when an AFP-producing tumour responds, but the expected kinetics depend on the clinical situation.\n\n### Is fasting required?\n\nUsually no.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for AFP: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [National Cancer Institute - Tumor Marker Tests in Common Use](https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/diagnosis-staging/diagnosis/tumor-markers-list)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
