    "excerpt": "Quick answer: Faecal calprotectin is a stool marker released by neutrophils during intestinal inflammation. It is useful for helping distinguish inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) from non-inflammatory disorders such as irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) in an appropriate clinical pathway, and for monitor",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Faecal calprotectin is a stool marker released by neutrophils during intestinal inflammation. It is useful for helping distinguish inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) from non-inflammatory disorders such as irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) in an appropriate clinical pathway, and for monitoring selected patients with known IBD.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Intestinal inflammation\n\n## What is the Faecal Calprotectin test?\n\nFaecal calprotectin is a stool marker released by neutrophils during intestinal inflammation. It is useful for helping distinguish inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) from non-inflammatory disorders such as irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) in an appropriate clinical pathway, and for monitoring selected patients with known IBD. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Persistent diarrhoea, abdominal pain or altered bowel habit when IBD is being considered\n- Helping decide who may need endoscopy or gastroenterology review\n- Monitoring intestinal inflammation in known Crohn disease or ulcerative colitis in selected care pathways\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nA small stool sample is collected in the laboratory-approved container. Avoid contamination with toilet water or urine. Medication use and recent infection should be reported. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and gastrointestinal infection can raise calprotectin in some patients.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nLow faecal calprotectin makes significant active intestinal inflammation less likely in the correct population. Elevated values indicate intestinal inflammation but do not identify the cause; IBD, infection, diverticular disease, colorectal neoplasia and other inflammatory conditions can raise it. Borderline results may be repeated depending on symptoms and local clinical pathways.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Not specific for Crohn disease or ulcerative colitis.\n- A raised value may occur with infection or other gastrointestinal inflammation.\n- Alarm features such as bleeding, unexplained weight loss, iron-deficiency anaemia or suspected cancer require direct clinical evaluation rather than relying on calprotectin alone.\n- Cut-offs vary by assay and pathway.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- CBC, CRP/ESR\n- Stool culture or pathogen testing when infection suspected\n- Celiac serology in selected patients\n- Endoscopy and imaging when clinically indicated\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What does a high faecal calprotectin mean?\n\nIt means there is evidence of intestinal neutrophilic inflammation, but it does not identify the exact cause.\n\n### Can IBS cause a high calprotectin?\n\nIBS itself is non-inflammatory and usually does not substantially raise calprotectin, but other coincidental conditions can.\n\n### Can infection raise calprotectin?\n\nYes.\n\n### Can NSAID painkillers affect the test?\n\nThey can raise calprotectin in some people; medication history should be considered.\n\n### Does a normal result completely rule out IBD?\n\nIt makes active IBD less likely but does not replace clinical assessment, especially when alarm features are present.\n\n### Is this a blood test?\n\nNo, it is measured in stool.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for Faecal Calprotectin: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- [NICE - Faecal calprotectin diagnostic guidance](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/dg11)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
