    "excerpt": "Quick answer: Tissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG-IgA) is the preferred first-line serologic test for celiac disease in most people. Total IgA is measured alongside it because IgA deficiency can produce a falsely negative IgA-based test. Accurate testing requires the patient to be eating gluten before ",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Tissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG-IgA) is the preferred first-line serologic test for celiac disease in most people. Total IgA is measured alongside it because IgA deficiency can produce a falsely negative IgA-based test. Accurate testing requires the patient to be eating gluten before and during the diagnostic evaluation.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Celiac disease screening\n\n## What is the tTG-IgA + Total IgA test?\n\nTissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG-IgA) is the preferred first-line serologic test for celiac disease in most people. Total IgA is measured alongside it because IgA deficiency can produce a falsely negative IgA-based test. Accurate testing requires the patient to be eating gluten before and during the diagnostic evaluation. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Chronic diarrhoea, bloating, weight loss or unexplained nutrient deficiency\n- Iron-deficiency anaemia without a clear cause\n- First-degree relative with celiac disease\n- Type 1 diabetes or other clinical situations with increased celiac risk\n- Monitoring selected patients after diagnosis\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nDo not start a gluten-free diet before testing unless advised by a specialist, because antibody levels can fall and make the result falsely reassuring. Blood sample; fasting is usually not required.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nA positive tTG-IgA in a person with adequate total IgA increases the likelihood of celiac disease and usually leads to specialist evaluation and, in adults, often duodenal biopsy confirmation. If total IgA is deficient, IgG-based tests such as tTG-IgG or DGP-IgG are used instead. A negative test while eating gluten makes celiac disease less likely but does not completely exclude it when clinical suspicion is high.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- A gluten-free diet before testing can cause false-negative serology.\n- IgA deficiency can make tTG-IgA falsely negative.\n- Very young children and mild disease may require adapted testing strategies.\n- Serology is not a substitute for clinical diagnosis and biopsy where indicated.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Total IgA\n- EMA-IgA\n- DGP-IgG or tTG-IgG when IgA deficient\n- CBC, ferritin, B12, folate, vitamin D and liver tests as clinically indicated\n- Duodenal biopsy\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why is total IgA tested with tTG-IgA?\n\nBecause IgA deficiency can make tTG-IgA falsely negative.\n\n### Should I stop gluten before the test?\n\nNo. For diagnostic accuracy you generally need to be eating gluten unless a specialist has given a different plan.\n\n### Does a positive tTG-IgA prove celiac disease?\n\nIt strongly supports the diagnosis, but confirmation strategy depends on age, antibody level and specialist guidance.\n\n### Can celiac disease exist with negative tTG-IgA?\n\nYes, particularly with IgA deficiency or in a minority of seronegative cases.\n\n### Is this an allergy test?\n\nNo. Celiac disease is an immune-mediated enteropathy, not a wheat IgE allergy.\n\n### Can the test be used after diagnosis?\n\nSerology can help monitor dietary adherence and response, although symptoms and nutritional recovery also matter.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for tTG-IgA + Total IgA: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [NIDDK - Celiac Disease Tests](https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/professionals/clinical-tools-patient-management/digestive-diseases/celiac-disease-health-care-professionals)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
