    "excerpt": "Quick answer: TB nucleic-acid amplification tests (NAATs), including cartridge-based platforms such as Xpert/CBNAAT, detect genetic material from Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex directly in clinical specimens. Many assays can also detect rifampicin resistance-associated mutations, allowing faster",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** TB nucleic-acid amplification tests (NAATs), including cartridge-based platforms such as Xpert/CBNAAT, detect genetic material from Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex directly in clinical specimens. Many assays can also detect rifampicin resistance-associated mutations, allowing faster diagnosis and treatment decisions than smear microscopy alone.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Tuberculosis molecular diagnosis\n\n## What is the TB PCR / CBNAAT test?\n\nTB nucleic-acid amplification tests (NAATs), including cartridge-based platforms such as Xpert/CBNAAT, detect genetic material from Mycobacterium tuberculosis complex directly in clinical specimens. Many assays can also detect rifampicin resistance-associated mutations, allowing faster diagnosis and treatment decisions than smear microscopy alone. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Suspected pulmonary tuberculosis\n- Selected extrapulmonary TB specimens according to validated assay and guideline\n- Rapid detection of rifampicin resistance\n- Upfront molecular testing in national TB diagnostic pathways\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nSpecimen quality is critical. Sputum should be collected according to laboratory instructions; other specimens such as CSF, lymph-node aspirate or tissue require specific containers and handling. A blood sample is generally not the routine specimen for diagnosing pulmonary TB by CBNAAT.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\n“MTB detected” indicates M. tuberculosis complex DNA was detected; the report may also state whether rifampicin resistance is detected, not detected or indeterminate. A negative result reduces the likelihood of TB but does not completely exclude disease, especially with paucibacillary disease or an inadequate specimen. Prior treatment can sometimes complicate interpretation because DNA may persist.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Sensitivity varies by specimen type and organism burden.\n- A negative NAAT does not always rule out TB.\n- Resistance results may require confirmation or additional drug-susceptibility testing depending on the pattern and programme guidance.\n- Culture remains important in selected cases for susceptibility testing and discordant results.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- AFB smear and culture\n- Chest imaging\n- Drug-susceptibility testing and line-probe assays\n- HIV testing when clinically appropriate\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent clinical review is appropriate for severe breathlessness, coughing up significant blood, confusion, severe weakness/dehydration, or rapid deterioration. Suspected infectious TB also requires prompt clinician/public-health follow-up to reduce transmission.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What is CBNAAT?\n\nIt is an automated cartridge-based nucleic-acid amplification test used to detect tuberculosis DNA and, on many platforms, rifampicin resistance markers.\n\n### Is CBNAAT the same as a TB blood test?\n\nNo. It usually tests sputum or another disease-site specimen, not blood.\n\n### Can TB PCR be negative even if I have TB?\n\nYes, particularly when few organisms are present or the specimen is suboptimal.\n\n### What does rifampicin resistance detected mean?\n\nIt suggests a molecular marker associated with rifampicin resistance and requires prompt TB-program/clinician management, often with additional resistance testing.\n\n### Does a positive PCR tell whether TB is active?\n\nIn a symptomatic patient with an appropriate specimen it supports active TB, but prior treatment and clinical context must be considered.\n\n### Is culture still needed?\n\nSometimes yes, especially for broader drug-susceptibility testing, treatment failure or discordant molecular results.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for TB PCR / CBNAAT: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- [WHO - Rapid diagnostics for tuberculosis detection](https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240089488)\n- [WHO - Current TB diagnostic classes and products](https://www.who.int/teams/global-programme-on-tuberculosis-and-lung-health/diagnosis-treatment/tb-diagnostic-classes-and-products)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
