    "excerpt": "Quick answer: ACTH is a pituitary hormone that stimulates the adrenal cortex to produce cortisol. Measuring ACTH together with cortisol helps localise disorders of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis—for example, distinguishing primary adrenal failure from pituitary or hypothalamic causes.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** ACTH is a pituitary hormone that stimulates the adrenal cortex to produce cortisol. Measuring ACTH together with cortisol helps localise disorders of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis—for example, distinguishing primary adrenal failure from pituitary or hypothalamic causes.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Pituitary-adrenal axis\n\n## What is the ACTH (Adrenocorticotropic Hormone) test?\n\nACTH is a pituitary hormone that stimulates the adrenal cortex to produce cortisol. Measuring ACTH together with cortisol helps localise disorders of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis—for example, distinguishing primary adrenal failure from pituitary or hypothalamic causes. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Suspected adrenal insufficiency with low cortisol\n- Evaluation of ACTH-dependent versus ACTH-independent cortisol excess\n- Assessment of pituitary-adrenal disorders\n- Selected dynamic endocrine tests\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nACTH is analytically fragile. It is commonly collected into EDTA plasma and requires prompt processing according to the laboratory protocol, often with cooling. Timing matters because ACTH and cortisol have circadian rhythms. Steroid medicines can alter the axis.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nLow cortisol with high ACTH supports primary adrenal insufficiency; low cortisol with low or inappropriately normal ACTH suggests secondary/tertiary adrenal insufficiency. In cortisol excess, ACTH helps determine whether the process is ACTH-dependent or ACTH-independent. Exact decision limits are assay- and protocol-specific.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Strict pre-analytical handling is essential.\n- Pulsatile and circadian secretion causes biologic variability.\n- A single ACTH should not be interpreted without cortisol and clinical context.\n- Exogenous glucocorticoids can suppress ACTH.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Morning cortisol\n- ACTH stimulation test\n- Dexamethasone suppression testing in cortisol excess\n- Electrolytes and glucose\n- Pituitary/adrenal imaging only after biochemical evaluation\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nSevere weakness with vomiting, dehydration, confusion, collapse or low blood pressure can be compatible with adrenal crisis and requires emergency treatment. Do not wait for outpatient test interpretation.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why are ACTH and cortisol ordered together?\n\nThe relationship between the two helps identify whether a problem is primarily in the adrenal gland or higher in the pituitary/hypothalamus.\n\n### Why does ACTH need special handling?\n\nThe peptide is unstable in blood, so collection and rapid processing affect accuracy.\n\n### Can stress affect ACTH?\n\nYes, physiologic stress can activate the pituitary-adrenal axis.\n\n### Does high ACTH always mean Addison disease?\n\nNo. It must be interpreted with cortisol and the clinical context.\n\n### Can steroid medicines lower ACTH?\n\nYes.\n\n### Is ACTH a routine wellness test?\n\nNo. It is a targeted endocrine investigation.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for ACTH: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [Endocrine Society - Primary Adrenal Insufficiency guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/primary-adrenal-insufficiency)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
