    "excerpt": "Quick answer: Parathyroid hormone (PTH) regulates calcium and phosphate balance. PTH should be interpreted together with calcium—preferably corrected or ionised when appropriate—plus phosphate, kidney function, magnesium and vitamin D. The key question is whether the PTH response is appropriate for ",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** Parathyroid hormone (PTH) regulates calcium and phosphate balance. PTH should be interpreted together with calcium—preferably corrected or ionised when appropriate—plus phosphate, kidney function, magnesium and vitamin D. The key question is whether the PTH response is appropriate for the calcium level.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Calcium, bone and parathyroid assessment\n\n## What is the PTH (Parathyroid Hormone) test?\n\nParathyroid hormone (PTH) regulates calcium and phosphate balance. PTH should be interpreted together with calcium—preferably corrected or ionised when appropriate—plus phosphate, kidney function, magnesium and vitamin D. The key question is whether the PTH response is appropriate for the calcium level. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- High or low blood calcium\n- Suspected primary or secondary hyperparathyroidism\n- Chronic kidney disease-mineral and bone disorder\n- Kidney stones, osteoporosis or unexplained fractures in selected patients\n- Monitoring after parathyroid treatment\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nBlood sample; fasting requirements vary by local protocol. Biotin can interfere with some immunoassays. Kidney function, vitamin D status, magnesium and calcium should be available for meaningful interpretation.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nHigh calcium with an inappropriately high or non-suppressed PTH suggests PTH-dependent hypercalcaemia such as primary hyperparathyroidism. Low calcium with high PTH can reflect secondary hyperparathyroidism due to vitamin D deficiency, chronic kidney disease or malabsorption. High calcium with suppressed PTH suggests a non-PTH cause and requires a different work-up.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- PTH assays detect different circulating fragments to varying degrees, especially in kidney disease.\n- Reference ranges are method-specific.\n- PTH cannot be interpreted properly without calcium and renal context.\n- Biotin may cause assay interference on some platforms.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Total and/or ionised calcium\n- Phosphate and magnesium\n- 25-OH vitamin D\n- Creatinine/eGFR\n- Urinary calcium in selected cases\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### What does high PTH mean?\n\nIt depends on calcium. High PTH with high calcium suggests a different problem from high PTH with low or normal calcium.\n\n### Can vitamin D deficiency raise PTH?\n\nYes. Low vitamin D can reduce calcium absorption and trigger secondary PTH elevation.\n\n### Can kidney disease raise PTH?\n\nYes, chronic kidney disease commonly causes secondary hyperparathyroidism.\n\n### Why do I need calcium with PTH?\n\nPTH is a calcium-regulating hormone; the appropriateness of PTH can only be judged against the calcium concentration.\n\n### Can biotin affect PTH testing?\n\nHigh-dose biotin can interfere with some immunoassays. Tell the laboratory about supplements.\n\n### Is PTH a cancer marker?\n\nNo. It is primarily a hormone test for calcium/parathyroid physiology.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for PTH: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
