    "excerpt": "Quick answer: The aldosterone-renin ratio compares aldosterone with renin and is the principal screening approach for primary aldosteronism, an important and treatable cause of hypertension. Accurate testing depends heavily on potassium level, sodium intake, posture, time of day and medications.",
    "content": "**Quick answer:** The aldosterone-renin ratio compares aldosterone with renin and is the principal screening approach for primary aldosteronism, an important and treatable cause of hypertension. Accurate testing depends heavily on potassium level, sodium intake, posture, time of day and medications.\n\n**Medically reviewed by:** Dr Shantakumar Muruda, MD (Medical Biochemistry), Consultant Biochemist — 25 years of experience · **Clinical area:** Primary aldosteronism screening\n\n## What is the Aldosterone-Renin Ratio (ARR) test?\n\nThe aldosterone-renin ratio compares aldosterone with renin and is the principal screening approach for primary aldosteronism, an important and treatable cause of hypertension. Accurate testing depends heavily on potassium level, sodium intake, posture, time of day and medications. The value of the test comes from using it for a specific clinical question and interpreting it with the patient’s history and other investigations. A laboratory number should not be interpreted in isolation.\n\n## Why might a doctor order it?\n\n- Hypertension where primary aldosteronism screening is indicated\n- Resistant or difficult-to-control hypertension\n- Hypertension with spontaneous or diuretic-associated low potassium\n- Hypertension with an adrenal incidentaloma or other high-risk clinical features\n\n## Sample, timing and preparation\n\nPreparation is critical. Current guidance recommends morning sampling under a standardised posture, avoiding unnecessary sodium restriction before screening and checking potassium because hypokalaemia can suppress aldosterone and create a false-negative result. Many antihypertensive medicines alter renin or aldosterone; medication adjustment must be clinician-supervised because stopping drugs can be unsafe.\n\n## How are results interpreted?\n\nPrimary aldosteronism is suggested by suppressed renin together with aldosterone that is inappropriately high for that renin level, producing an elevated ARR. The ratio should never be interpreted without the individual aldosterone and renin values, units and assay method. Positive screens may lead to confirmatory testing or, in selected high-probability cases, direct subtype evaluation and treatment planning.\n\n## Important limitations and sources of misinterpretation\n\n- Medication effects are a major source of false-positive and false-negative results.\n- Low potassium can falsely lower aldosterone.\n- ARR cut-offs vary with renin method, aldosterone assay and units.\n- A high ratio caused only by extremely low renin with low aldosterone may not indicate primary aldosteronism.\n- Always use the laboratory’s own report, method-specific reference interval and interpretive comments. Do not transfer a cut-off from another laboratory or online article without confirming that the assay and clinical pathway are comparable.\n\n## Related tests or next-step investigations\n\n- Serum potassium and sodium\n- Creatinine/eGFR\n- Confirmatory suppression testing when indicated\n- Adrenal CT and adrenal venous sampling only after biochemical diagnosis and specialist assessment\n\n## When should a patient seek urgent medical care?\n\nUrgent care is determined by symptoms, not by waiting for a laboratory report. Seek immediate medical assessment for severe chest pain or pressure, major breathing difficulty, fainting, confusion, shock/very low blood pressure, uncontrolled bleeding, rapidly worsening weakness, or any other severe acute illness. A laboratory test page must never delay emergency care.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### Why test for primary aldosteronism?\n\nIt is a potentially treatable cause of hypertension associated with excess cardiovascular risk if missed.\n\n### Can blood-pressure medicines affect ARR?\n\nYes. Several classes alter renin and/or aldosterone; interpretation or medication adjustment should be managed by a clinician.\n\n### Why is potassium checked?\n\nLow potassium can suppress aldosterone and make the screen falsely reassuring.\n\n### Should I reduce salt before the test?\n\nCurrent guidance advises against unnecessary sodium restriction before screening; follow the ordering clinician’s protocol.\n\n### Does a high ARR prove primary aldosteronism?\n\nNo. It is a screening result that must be interpreted with absolute aldosterone and renin values and the clinical context.\n\n### Is the test done lying down or sitting?\n\nPosture affects both hormones. Follow the exact laboratory/clinician protocol so the result is interpretable.\n\n## Booking at QXL Diagnostics, Bengaluru\n\nSpecimen type, method, reference interval, turnaround time and price for Aldosterone-Renin Ratio: check with QXL for more information. See the [QXL test directory](/tests), [book a test or home collection](/book), or [WhatsApp QXL](https://wa.me/919964639639).\n\n*This page is for patient education. It does not diagnose disease, replace a clinical examination, or override the reference interval and interpretive comment on your own laboratory report. Reference intervals, decision limits, specimen requirements and assay interferences can differ by method, age, sex, pregnancy status, clinical setting and laboratory platform.*\n\n## Evidence base for this article\n\n- Tietz Clinical Guide to Laboratory Tests, 4th ed. Alan H. B. Wu. W.B. Saunders/Elsevier, 2006.\n- [Endocrine Society - Primary Aldosteronism guideline resources](https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/primary-aldosteronism-2)\n\n*Original patient-facing synthesis; not copied from Tietz or from guideline text. Where the 2006 Tietz edition differs from current practice, current clinical guidance takes precedence.*\n",
